  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1119 PM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nSummer heat persists across the region thanks to upper-level\nridging. In accordance with 00Z surface analysis, a back door\ncold front resides just south of the Tennessee and Mississippi\nstate line. This front brought convection to north Mississippi\nand eastern Arkansas Sunday afternoon allowing for brief segments\nof relief from heat, but didn't help much in the way of drought\nworsening conditions. Dry air and cold air advection trail behind\nthe aforementioned front allowing for lowered dewpoints in the\nupper 50s to lower 60s to accompany highs in the mid to upper 90s\nfor Labor Day. Heat indices will be in the lower 100s, below the\n105+F Heat Advisory threshold, across the Mid-South. Minimum\nrelative humidity values will fall below 30% as lowered dewpoints\nfilter in resulting in at least marginal fire weather concerns\nMonday.\n\nUpper-level ridging, currently centered over the southern Central\nPlains, will continue to build back over the Mississippi Valley,\ncentering over us by Tuesday. Temperatures will increase slightly\nTuesday with highs in the mid 90s to lower 100s, ranging about\n10 degrees above normal for this time of the year. This trend\nwill continue through Wednesday as upper-level ridging persists\nacross the region. Come Thursday, ensembles continue in decent\nagreement with an upper-level low pushing over eastern Canada\nwith weak troughing dipping into the Upper-Mississippi Valley. An\nattendant cold front and decent height falls will bring our next\nshot at appreciable rainfall Thursday into early next weekend.\nThough exact timing of this front and heightfalls remain hard to\npinpoint this far out, we will welcome any precipitation to\nhopefully help alleviate worsening drought conditions. The latest\nLREF has around a 25% chance for >500 J/kg of SBCAPE and 20kts of\n0-6km bulk wind shear Thursday and Friday. Severe weather may be\na concern with a front amidst persistent daytime heating both\ndays, however, more refined details will be ironed out as time\ndraws closer. Post frontal temperatures look to drop about 2-\n4 degrees with highs in the low to mid 90s by late next week,\nstill above normal for the 2nd week of September. The latest CPC\n8-14 day outlook has us highlighted 50-60% above normal for\ntemperatures and leaning below normal for precipitation.\n\nAEH",
